<main><article class="editorial-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">Tour de France · Mulhouse 1970</p><h1>He crossed the line first.<br/><em>The other man won.</em></h1><p>Joaquim Agostinho reached for team-mate Mogens Frey during the sprint. The jury saw what the spectators had seen.</p></header><figure class="story-image hero"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Mogens Frey riding the individual pursuit at the 1967 World Championships in Amsterdam" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Mogens Frey in the individual pursuit at the 1967 World Championships in Amsterdam. Three years later he took Denmark's first Tour stage after an unusual team sprint. Photo: Eric Koch/Anefo, CC0 via Nationaal Archief and Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">Frey tried to pass on the right. Agostinho had other plans and did not rely on his bicycle alone to close the door.</p><h2>From Saarlouis to Mulhouse</h2><p>Stage nine of the 1970 Tour, on 5 July, covered 273.5 kilometres from Saarlouis to Mulhouse and crossed the Col de la Schlucht. Eddy Merckx wore yellow and already controlled the race. Smaller teams had to look for their chances in breakaways.</p><p>Mogens Frey was 29 and a new professional. He was not new to winning. On the track he had taken Olympic team pursuit gold and individual pursuit silver in Mexico City in 1968, then won the individual pursuit world title. The road and the Tour demanded a different kind of work.</p><p>He rode for Frimatic–De Gribaldy. The team's leading name was Portugal's Joaquim Agostinho, who had won a Tour stage in Mulhouse the previous year. Frey was the new man. Agostinho was the captain.</p><h2>Two men from one team</h2><p>Frey escaped during the stage. After the descent from the Col de la Schlucht, Agostinho joined him. The team-mates kept the peloton behind. Later accounts say that with roughly fifty kilometres remaining, an exhausted Frey asked Agostinho to do most of the work.</p><p>The story becomes less secure here. Some later versions portray Frey as a passenger who refused to work despite instructions from the team car. Others stress that he had created the break and had simply run low on strength. What is certain is that the two reached Mulhouse together with the peloton close behind.</p><p>A team might have settled the finishing order. The captain had done much of the late work and had won here before. Frey had his own opportunity. Once the sprint began, no agreement remained visible.</p><h2>The hand on the bars</h2><p>Agostinho tried to keep Frey against the barriers. Photographs and accounts of the finish show the Portuguese rider extending a hand towards the Dane's handlebars and holding him back. Agostinho crossed the line first.</p><p>It was not a subtle offence. The jury relegated Agostinho and awarded the stage to Frey. Both were given 7 hours, 44 minutes and 14 seconds. Marino Basso led the peloton home only three seconds later. After more than seven hours of racing, the decision turned on a movement lasting an instant.</p><p>The victory made Frey the first Danish stage winner in Tour de France history. Thirteen years passed before Kim Andersen took the next Danish stage in 1983.</p><h2>Was it a stolen victory?</h2><p>The episode is often reduced to a dispute between an exhausted captain and an ungrateful helper. That version offers a neat moral, but racing is rarely that orderly. Frey was a world champion in his own right, and a Tour stage could change a new professional's career. Agostinho had spent energy keeping the peloton away and plainly expected Mulhouse to be his again.</p><p>We know the jury's decision, the result and the visible obstruction. Later accounts cannot tell us exactly what was said in the car or between the riders. Jean de Gribaldy's circle later maintained that the dispute was quickly put aside and that the two shared a room again a few days later. That is a later recollection, not part of the official result.</p><h2>A win without the usual finish photograph</h2><p>Frey completed the Tour in 73rd place. Agostinho was 14th overall. Merckx won the race and eight stages. Their argument in Mulhouse was a small incident in the wider story of the 1970 Tour.</p><p>In Danish cycling it endured. The country's first Tour stage did not end with raised arms and a clear wheel's advantage. Frey crossed second, waited for the jury and was entered as the winner. Agostinho's hand had decided the sprint — simply not in the way he intended.</p></div>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>The stage ran from Saarlouis to Mulhouse, not Vittel as stated in the original story proposal. Details about the work-sharing and later room-sharing come from later retellings and are identified as such.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.letour.fr/en/history">Tour de France: historical results</a></li><li><a href="https://bikeraceinfo.com/tdf/tdf1970.html">BikeRaceInfo: 1970 Tour, stage nine</a></li><li><a href="https://www.jeandegribaldy.com/index.php?zone=pages/equ_70b">Jean de Gribaldy archive: Frimatic 1970 and the Mulhouse finish</a></li><li><a href="https://www.lequipe.fr/Cyclisme-sur-route/Article/Histoires-danoises-1-4-mogens-frey-drole-de-premiere-sur-le-tour-de-france/1341015">L'Équipe: Mogens Frey's first Danish Tour stage</a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WK_Wielrennen,_series_achtervolging_amateurs._De_Deen_Mogens_Frey_in_aktie,_Bestanddeelnr_920-6322.jpg">Wikimedia Commons: Mogens Frey 1967</a></li></ul><p><strong>Alternative illustration:</strong> A clearly labelled illustration of the 1970 Mulhouse sprint from a rear three-quarter angle: two Frimatic riders on steel racing bicycles beside the barriers, one reaching towards the other's handlebars; no invented faces or modern equipment.</p></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav" aria-label="Continue browsing"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/stories/niels-fredborg-mexico-1968/`}>Fredborg in Mexico →</a></nav></article></main><footer><div class="brand footer-brand"><span class="brand-mark">E<span>B</span></span><span>The Evolution<br/>of Bikes</span></div><p>A personal collection of vintage bicycles,<br/>stories, brands and components.</p><p class="copyright">© {new Date().getFullYear()} The Evolution of Bikes</p></footer></body></html>
